    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d933">

                <pb facs="0748" n="1866" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p2/pg1866"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.95B/8–2054: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Embassy in Korea</hi><anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d933-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-08-20T19:19:00-04:00">August 20, 1954—7:19
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>148. Tokyo pass CINCUNC. Your 208 rptd Tokyo
                        132.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d933-f2"/> In Ambassador Briggs’ discussion with Secretary and Robertson this morning decision taken continue
                    seek ROK Government signature to draft
                        Minute<anchor n="3" xml:id="d933-f3"/> before making any final commitments
                    on US military economic aid programs. Some modifications may be desirable in
                    light appropriation bills for example specifying amounts to be made available.
                    This along lines recommendation reftel.</p>
                <p>Instructions now in preparation for Ambassador Briggs, who leaves Washington tomorrow, to take up this matter
                    with President Rhee soonest after his
                    return Seoul.</p>
                <p>FOA and Defense will require about ten days
                    apportioning appropriation among several countries concerned before definite
                    figures can be developed on Korea. Stassen so informed Paek
                    this morning and latter will apparently remain Washington for present.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d933-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d933-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram, drafted by Young and
                        Briggs and cleared with Wood of FOA,
                    was repeated to Tokyo as 391.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d933-f2" n="2" xml:id="d933-f2-content">
                <p>In this telegram, Aug. 20, Strom reported
                    that the Embassy staff believed that ROK formal
                    agreement to the draft minute worked out in Washington during the Rhee visit of July 26–30 should be obtained
                    immediately in light of Rhee’s past
                    tendencies of avoiding written commitments while insisting on U.S. Government
                    fulfillment of its assurances concerning miltary and economic programs.
                    (795B.5/8–2054)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d933-f3" n="3" xml:id="d933-f3-content">
                <p>For text of the draft minute, see <ref target="#pg1859">p. 1859</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
